  "title": "Sounds, greetings & polite address",
  "focus": "Greet people at any time of day, introduce yourself, address people respectfully (Bapak/Ibu), church greetings, and the first Malay → Indonesian traps.",
  "lesson": {
    "title": "Week 1 · Bunyi & salam (Sounds & greetings)",
    "sections": [
      {
        "h": "How Indonesian sounds",
        "p": ["Indonesian is spelled almost exactly as it sounds, so once you know a handful of rules you can read anything aloud. In the pronunciation hints, CAPITALS mark a light stress — Indonesian stress is gentle, so don't punch it."],
        "list": [
          "c is always \"ch\": cari = CHAH-ree, bicara = bee-CHAH-rah",
          "e is usually a soft \"uh\": enam = uh-NAHM, selamat = suh-LAH-maht. Sometimes it's \"eh\": sore = SO-reh. Learn it word by word.",
          "ng is the sound in \"sing\" — even at the start: nggak = ngahk",
          "ny is the sound in \"canyon\": banyak = BAH-nyahk",
          "r is a light roll or tap, never the English \"rr\"",
          "kh sounds like a soft throat \"h\"; final k is almost silent: Bapak = BAH-pah(k)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "h": "Greetings follow the clock",
        "list": [
          "Selamat pagi — morning, until about 11am",
          "Selamat siang — late morning to about 3pm",
          "Selamat sore — about 3pm until dusk",
          "Selamat malam — evening and night"
        ],
        "p": ["At church on Sunday morning you'll hear \"Selamat pagi, Bapak-Ibu sekalian\" — good morning, everyone. Many Indonesian Christians also greet each other with \"Syalom\"."]
      },
      {
        "h": "Respect: use the title instead of \"you\"",
        "p": [
          "Indonesians often avoid saying \"you\" to an older or respected person. Instead they use the title: \"Bapak dari mana?\" literally \"Where is Sir from?\" means \"Where are you from?\" Use Bapak (Pak) for men and Ibu (Bu) for women — pastors, elders, hosts, anyone older.",
          "Anda is the polite \"you\" for strangers and formal settings. Kamu is for friends and people younger than you. Mas and Mbak are friendly titles for young men and women — waiters, drivers, shop staff.",
          "For ministry, default to Bapak/Ibu and Anda. It's always safe to be too polite."
        ]
      },
      {
        "h": "Malay traps (because you live in Singapore)",
        "p": ["Your Malay helps, but some words differ or even mean something else in Indonesia. This week's traps are marked with a red tag. The big ones: kereta is a train (car = mobil), and percuma means \"pointless\" (free = gratis)."]
      }
    ]
  },
